' --- manual: everything below this line is hand-authored; the script preserves it ---

' --- What the registrar composes, and what it does not ---

' register_handlers binds three handler families and nothing else:
'
'   workflow.v1.ore.import               -> ore_import_handler
'   ore.v1.ore.import.{execute,rollback} -> ore_import_execute_handler
'   ore.v1.report.prepare-package        -> report_package_handler
'
' The entity's own protocol family is not bound here. The generated
' series_key_shape handler, repository, service and event mapping exist, are
' compiled, and are composed nowhere in the tree, so the nine
' ore.v1.series_key_shapes* subjects answer nothing. The one real consumer of
' the entity reaches its repository directly instead.

ores::ore::service::messaging::registrar ..> ores::ore::service::messaging::ore_import_handler : binds
ores::ore::service::messaging::registrar ..> ores::ore::service::messaging::ore_import_execute_handler : binds
ores::ore::service::messaging::registrar ..> ores::ore::service::messaging::report_package_handler : binds

' register_series_key_shape_event_mapping exists in the messaging facet and is
' absent from this picture because the automated pass sees only classes, not
' free functions.
note right of "ores::ore::service::messaging::registrar"
    Composed once, from application::run. It binds the handlers the
    service serves; a family it does not bind is served by nobody.
    register_series_key_shape_event_mapping is generated by
    cpp_nats_event_registrar.hpp.mustache, is compiled, and is
    called from nowhere, so no event mapping is installed at startup.
end note

note right of "ores::ore::service::app::application"
    Reads config, builds the database context and the NATS client,
    then hands both to registrar::register_handlers. Every handler
    family the service serves is wired here or not at all.
end note

